We examine Neural Networks (NNs) for keyword spotting on devices such as smart speakers and wearables. Our focus is on NN execution within devices with limited resources; thus, we suggest a Compact Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architecture. This CNN features (i) reduced computation requirements via limited multiplications. (ii) an economical use of model parameters to minimize memory usage. (iii) an adaptation to device audio input variability. (iv) an enhanced robustness to class-balanced data. Our experiments on datasets like AudioSet and VoiceCommand show the CNN's superiority over established NN architectures like ResNet, FCN, and GoogLeNet. Additionally, we tested the CNN on real-world smart speaker and wearable devices, demonstrating its efficiency with keywords detected in under 100 milliseconds, and an overall system latency of 250 milliseconds or less. This CNN code is available at https://github.com/ali-hoseini/Keyword_Spoting_CNN.git. {"https://github.com/ali-hoseini/Keyword_Spoting_CNN.git" as @github] is an open source project.} {"https://github.com/ali-hoseini/Keyword_Spoting_CNN.git" as @github] is an open source project.} 

